# Line endings are a correctness issue in this repo, not a style preference.
#
# client/install-bridge.sh and client/setup.sh are fetched over HTTPS from whatever host
# serves the client bundle and run with /bin/sh. On Debian and Ubuntu that is
# dash, and dash does not strip a trailing carriage return: a CRLF file makes the
# very first line fail with "set: Illegal option -" and the installer aborts
# before doing anything. This has already happened once and was repaired by hand
# on the host, which is exactly the kind of undocumented manual fix this repo
# exists to eliminate.
#
# The scripts are authored on Windows, so any clone with core.autocrlf=true
# would silently reintroduce the fault on checkout. These rules pin the working
# tree to LF regardless of the cloner's local git configuration, which is the one
# guarantee that matters now that nothing re-checks the bytes at publish time.
#
# After adding or changing this file, run once, before committing:
#     git add --renormalize .
# Without that, files already staged with CRLF keep their stored line endings.

# Default for anything not named below. Git decides what is text; text is LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit rules for every text type actually present in the tree. Named
# individually so a new file type does not quietly inherit an untested default.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.nginx     text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.example   text eol=lf

# PowerShell is LF here too, deliberately. Windows PowerShell 5.1 and
# PowerShell 7 both read LF scripts correctly, and the copies served to
# workstations are already LF. Keeping one rule for the whole tree avoids a second
# class of file that the CR guards would have to exempt.
*.ps1       text eol=lf

# Binary: never line-ending converted, never diffed as text.
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
